A compact way to write the market test is $\text{customer choice}
=
f(\text{trust},\text{offer},\text{execution},\text{price},\text{timing}).$ Age can enter the transaction only if it changes one of those variables, especially trust. In Mitt’s telling, the young founder’s mistake is to let age become a reason before the market has even rendered judgment.

